  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEKEND/... \nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nMonday-Thursday:\nAs we progress into Monday, the ridge will be displaced further\nwest, leaving our area on the eastern periphery. This will\nsupport slight cooler temperatures than the previous week for\nsome, with the extreme heat persisting elsewhere. Across the\neastern half of the area, expect highs in the lower to middle\n90s. Meanwhile, upper 90s to around 100 persist across the\nwestern half of the area. This will once again support heat\nindex values around 105, providing high enough confidence to\nextend the Extreme Warning for counties west of Highway 65.\nCounties to the east will see heat index values top out in the\nupper 90s to around 100. At this time, confidence is not high\nenough to warrant a heat product. The ridge subtly shifts back \neast into Tuesday and Wednesday, with upper 90s to lower 100s \nreturning to the entire area. This will likely warrant \nadditional heat headlines as progress into early week.\n\nAdditionally, the area remains dry through Monday and Tuesday, \nahead of pattern change late Wednesday into Thursday. The upper-\nlevel ridge flattens across the central CONUS, allowing a \nfrontal passage to sweep through the region. NBM PoPs have \ncontinued to increase for Wednesday evening/night into Thursday,\nupwards of 50-80% along the front. Ensembles are coming into \nbetter agreement on at least some beneficial rainfall. Though \nthere is still some uncertainty on the exact corridor of highest\nrainfall amounts. NBM depicts the following probabilities:\n\nProb of > 0.25 inch: 50-80%\nProb of > 0.50 inch: 40-70%\nProb of > 1.00 inch: 20-40%\n\nSubsequent forecasts will work to pin down rainfall amounts.\nNonetheless, any rainfall will be beneficial given the \nworsening drought conditions, further amplified by the extreme \nheat.\n\nNext Weekend:\nEnsemble guidance diverges a bit into next week, but the general\ntrend support a bit cooler temperatures, yet still 5 to 10 \ndegrees above normal for early to mid September. This would \ncorrelate to highs in the middle 80s to lower 90s. Additional \nshortwaves translate through the flow, supporting low rain \nchances (10-30%).",
